Perform a read-only umbrella application-security review of this bounded import change and trace only concrete reachable cross-boundary issues. Intended behavior: an authenticated tenant member uploads a `.tar` archive; the API stores it under that tenant’s prefix and a worker extracts it into the tenant’s import directory. Changed paths and diff:

`api/import.ts:41-49`
```diff
+const name = file.originalname;
+if (!/\.tar$/.test(name)) throw new BadRequest('tar required');
+const objectKey = `${req.user.tenantId}/${name}`;
+await bucket.put(objectKey, file.buffer);
+await imports.enqueue({ tenantId: req.user.tenantId, objectKey });
```

`worker/extract.ts:18-23`
```diff
+const localPath = `/work/${job.objectKey}`;
+await bucket.download(job.objectKey, localPath);
+execSync(`tar -xf ${localPath} -C /work/${job.tenantId}/out`);
```

Identity and reachability facts: tenant member `member-17` in tenant `t-olive` is permitted by `requireTenantMember` to call `POST /imports`; the queue runs as `import-worker`, which has read access to the shared import bucket for all tenant prefixes. The raw `originalname` is preserved in `objectKey` and the queue payload. The extension check accepts `x$(touch${IFS}pwned).tar`; no subsequent normalization, shell escaping, or argv-based process API appears in the supplied path. Connected boundaries are attacker-controlled multipart metadata, authenticated tenant identity, tenant-prefixed object storage, a system-worker identity, shell command construction, and archive extraction. Data classification: imported archives can contain confidential tenant invoices. Deployment boundary: the API and worker are separate pods in a multi-tenant production namespace; the worker uses a shared bucket credential but writes extraction output under the queued tenant directory. Available negative evidence: `api/import.test.ts` shows `notes.txt` is rejected and `orders.tar` is accepted, but contains no shell-metacharacter case; `worker/extract.test.ts` asserts the interpolated command for `orders.tar` and provides no rejection or escaping check. Review the supplied path only, identify the violated invariant and plausible impact where supported, account for current defenses, and give the smallest safe regression scenario. Do not modify code, execute the payload, probe a live system, access tenant data, or approve release.
